// Where a subtree's bytes actually live, as a value rather than as a tag.
//
// **This file is the whole of this rewrite, and it needs nothing from vision.** The shipped
// `packages/fs/src/fs.wac` opens by explaining why it is one concrete type with a mount table and a
// hand-written branch, and the explanation is two claims:
//
//   > wac will not do it: `override` is a source-level check and **dispatch is static** — a `Circle`
//   > in a `Shape` variable answers `Shape.name()` — so a base-typed `Fs` would always run the
//   > base's bodies. The language's own idiom for varying behaviour is a funcref plus explicit state
//   > (`Shell.external` is one), and **a funcref cannot capture a filesystem because there are no
//   > closures**.
//
// Measured, both, on 2026-09-04:
//
//     dispatch: static      a Circle in a Shape variable answers 1, not 2
//     capture:  works       a lambda reading an enclosing struct, stored in a funcref field, called
//                           through it, returns what the captured field says
//
// **The first claim holds and the second does not.** Lambdas landed on 2026-08-16 —
// `spec/cases/0188` and the eleven cases after it, and `0191 a lambda captures by reference` is the
// one that settles this. `fs.wac` was written on 2026-08-05, when the sentence was true, and was
// last edited on 2026-08-30, fourteen days after it stopped being. `packages/fs/src` contains no
// lambda anywhere.
//
// So the mount table is a workaround for a constraint that half-lifted three weeks ago, and the
// design the header says it wanted — *"a funcref plus explicit state"* — is writable today with the
// state captured rather than explicit. That is a finding about the shipped language, not about
// vision, and it is the most useful thing this rewrite produced.
//
// ## What the closure changes
//
// The shipped `Fs` branches on a `Backing` enum at every operation, so every operation knows about
// every backing: adding a third means editing `readFile`, `writeFile`, `stat`, `readDir`, `mkdir`,
// `remove`, `rename` and the rest. A `Mount` of funcrefs inverts it — a backing is built once, in
// one place, and the operations do not know how many there are.
//
// The cost is real and is the usual one: a funcref field per operation is a word per operation per
// mount, against one tag for all of them, and a call through a funcref is not inlined where a
// branch on a tag might be. A filesystem is not an inner loop — `vision/bench/slicecost.wac` puts a
// per-call cost at nanoseconds and a `readFile` is microseconds at best — so this is the case where
// that trade is obviously right, and saying so is the point: it is not right everywhere and
// `gzip`'s window is where it is wrong.
//
// ## The slot type is the ticket, not `async`, and I got that wrong first
//
// These fields were written `fn<async Result<Bytes, Fault>(string)>` and the parser refused them
// within a second of the file being saved. The refusal was right and there is a tagged claim for it:
// `[§wac-async-lambda-slot-9wq4nkz]` — *"a lambda writes no return type, so `fn[Pending<R>(…)]` is
// what permits `async` and names `R`"*. `async` is a property of a *definition*; a funcref type says
// what it answers, and what an async function answers is a ticket.
//
// Worth recording as another instance of this directory's worst habit — inventing a spelling for
// something that already has one — and the first one an instrument caught. The seven before it
// survived into a README and were found by reading the spec afterwards.
//
// **And it leaves a real complaint rather than an error.** `fn<Ticket<Result<Bytes, Fault>>(string)>`
// is three type constructors deep to say *reads a path, may fail*, and there are six of them below.
// That is a legibility cost with no cure in this file, and it is in `../../../QUESTIONS.md`.

import { Result, Ticket, Bytes } from "core";
import { Files } from "std";
import { Fault, NotFound, ReadOnly, Unsupported } from "./fault.wac";

export struct Entry { string name; bool isDir; i64 size; i64 modified; }

/**
 * One subtree's operations, closed over whatever backs them.
 *
 * Every field is a funcref rather than a method, which is the difference: a method would dispatch on
 * `Mount`'s own type and there is only one of those. What varies is the lambda in the field.
 */
export struct Mount {
  fn<Ticket<Result<Bytes, Fault>>(string)> read;
  fn<Ticket<Result<void, Fault>>(string, Bytes)> write;
  fn<Ticket<Result<Entry, Fault>>(string)> stat;
  fn<Ticket<Result<Entry[], Fault>>(string)> readDir;
  fn<Ticket<Result<void, Fault>>(string, bool)> mkdir;
  fn<Ticket<Result<void, Fault>>(string, bool)> remove;

  /**
   * A mount backed by this program's own memory: the default, and what a test wants.
   *
   * `tree` is captured, not stored on the `Mount`, which is the whole trick. The shipped design has
   * nowhere to put it except on `Fs` itself, so `Fs` holds the memory of every memory mount and
   * hands the right one to each branch by index.
   */
  Mount inMemory() {
    Map<string, Node> tree = Map.create();
    return Mount(
      (string p) => memRead(tree, p),
      (string p, Bytes b) => memWrite(tree, p, b),
      (string p) => memStat(tree, p),
      (string p) => memReadDir(tree, p),
      (string p, bool parents) => memMkdir(tree, p, parents),
      (string p, bool recursive) => memRemove(tree, p, recursive),
    );
  }

  /**
   * A mount backed by the host, through the capability the caller was given.
   *
   * **`Files`, not `Sys`.** This is the projection's first real consumer and the one place it has to
   * pay for itself: a filesystem needs paths and nothing else, and the type now says so. The shipped
   * equivalent takes a whole `Cli`, so `Fs.onHost` holds a capability that can open a socket.
   */
  Mount onHost(Files files) {
    return Mount(
      (string p) => files.read(p),
      (string p, Bytes b) => files.write(p, b),
      (string p) => hostStat(files, p),
      (string p) => hostReadDir(files, p),
      (string p, bool parents) => files.mkdir(p, parents),
      (string p, bool recursive) => files.remove(p, recursive),
    );
  }

  /**
   * A mount that answers every read and refuses every write.
   *
   * Three lines, and the shipped design has no way to express it: read-only is a `bool` on the mount
   * that eight operations each have to remember to check. Here it is a wrapper, and an operation
   * that forgot would have to be written to forget.
   */
  Mount readOnly(Mount inner) {
    return Mount(
      inner.read,
      (string p, Bytes b) => Err(ReadOnly(p)),
      inner.stat,
      inner.readDir,
      (string p, bool parents) => Err(ReadOnly(p)),
      (string p, bool recursive) => Err(ReadOnly(p)),
    );
  }

  /** Nothing is here and nothing can be put here. The mount a sealed session gets. */
  Mount empty() {
    return Mount(
      (string p) => Err(NotFound(p)),
      (string p, Bytes b) => Err(ReadOnly(p)),
      (string p) => Err(NotFound(p)),
      (string p) => Err(NotFound(p)),
      (string p, bool parents) => Err(ReadOnly(p)),
      (string p, bool recursive) => Err(NotFound(p)),
    );
  }
}

struct Node { bool isDir; Bytes bytes; i64 modified; }

async Result<Bytes, Fault> memRead(Map<string, Node> tree, string path) { … }
async Result<void, Fault> memWrite(Map<string, Node> tree, string path, Bytes b) { … }
async Result<Entry, Fault> memStat(Map<string, Node> tree, string path) { … }
async Result<Entry[], Fault> memReadDir(Map<string, Node> tree, string path) { … }
async Result<void, Fault> memMkdir(Map<string, Node> tree, string path, bool parents) { … }
async Result<void, Fault> memRemove(Map<string, Node> tree, string path, bool recursive) { … }

async Result<Entry, Fault> hostStat(Files files, string path) { … }
async Result<Entry[], Fault> hostReadDir(Files files, string path) { … }
